verses.text:
And he said,
“Naked I came out of my mother’s womb,
and naked shall I return there.{j}
Yahweh gave, and Yahweh has taken;
let the name of Yahweh be blessed.”

Let darkness and deep shadow{a} claim it as their own;
let a cloud settle upon it;
let the blackenings of the day{b} terrify it.

That night—let thick darkness seize it!
Let it not rejoice among the days of the year;
into the number of the months let it not come.

Let the stars of its twilight be dark;
let it wait for light, but have none,
nor let it see the eyelids of the dawn,

It stood still, but I could not discern its appearance;
a form was before my eyes;
there was silence, and then I heard a voice:

His harvest the hungry eat up,
and even from among thorns they take it,{b}
and the thirsty{c} pant after their wealth.

For the arrows of Shaddai are within me;
their poison my spirit drinks;
the terrors of God are arrayed against me.

When I lie down I say, ‘At what hour may I rise?’
But the evening drags on,{a}
and I am glutted with restless turnings till the twilight of dawn.

“Therefore I also will not restrain my mouth;
I will speak in the distress of my spirit;
I will complain in the bitterness of my soul.

if you are pure and upright,
surely now he will rouse himself for you
and restore the habitation of your righteousness.

The earth is given into the hand of the wicked;
the faces of its judges he covers.
If it is not he, then who is it?

“My soul is weary of my life;
I will give free course to my complaint;
I will speak in the bitterness of my soul.

You renew your witnesses against me
and increase your vexation toward me;
changes and a host are with me.{a}

a land of gloom like thick darkness,{c}
of deep shadow without any order,
where the light is as thick darkness.’ ”

and declare to you the secrets of wisdom!
For sound wisdom{b} is double-sided.
Know then that God exacts of you less than your iniquity deserves.{c}

But the eyes of the wicked shall fail;
refuge has perished from them,
and their hope is the breathing out of the soul.”

But I too have a mind, as well as you;
I fall not beneath you;
and who knows not such things as these?

A laughingstock to his friend I am become,
one who called upon God and he answered him;
a laughingstock is the righteous, the blameless man.

At peace stand the tents of plunderers,
and secure are they who rouse God to anger,
he that carries a god in his own fist.{b}

You put my feet in the stocks
and watch all my paths;
you set a mark{d} about the soles of my feet.

“For there is hope for a tree:
if it is cut down, it will sprout again,
and its tender shoot will not cease.

so a man lies down and rises not up;
until the heavens be no more they shall not wake,
nor be stirred up from their slumber.

waters wear away the stones;
their torrents wash away the soil of the earth;
so you destroy the hope of man.

and dwelt in cities laid waste,
in houses none should inhabit,
which were destined to become heaps;

He shall not depart out of darkness;
the flame shall dry up his shoot,
and by the breath of his{f} mouth he shall pass away.

His anger has torn me and borne a grudge against me;
he has gnashed upon me with his teeth;
my adversary sharpens his eyes against me.

They have gaped upon me with their mouth;
with reproach they have struck my cheeks;
together they mass themselves against me.

I was at ease, and he shattered me;
he seized me by the neck and dashed me to pieces;
he set me up as his target;

his archers{b} surround me.
He splits open my kidneys and does not spare;
he pours out my gall upon the ground.

You who tear yourself in your anger,
for your sake shall the earth be forsaken,
or the rock be moved out of its place?

His troops come on together;
they have built up their way against me
and encamp round about my tent.

whom I myself shall see for myself,
and my own eyes shall behold, and not a stranger.{i}
My kidneys{j} are spent within my bosom.

be afraid of the sword for yourselves;
for wrath brings the punishments of the sword,
that you may know there is a judgment.”{l}

To fill his belly to the full,
God{c} shall send the burning of his anger upon him
and rain it upon him as his food.{d}

He draws it, and it comes out from his back;
the flashing blade, out from his liver;
upon him are terrors.

All darkness is laid up for his treasures;
a fire not blown upon shall devour him;
it shall consume what is left in his tent.

Sweet to him are the clods of the valley;
and after him all mankind draws on,
as before him there were without number.

See, like wild donkeys in the wilderness
they go forth to their toil, seeking eagerly for food;
the desert yields bread for their children.

Out of the city the dying make their moan,
and the wounded soul shouts for aid;
yet God lays no charge of folly.{d}

“These are among those who rebel against the light;
they do not know its ways
nor stay in its paths.

At the light the murderer rises;
he kills the poor and needy,
and in the night he is like the thief.

In darkness men break through into houses;
by daytime they seal themselves in;
light they know not.

“Swift is he{e} upon the face of the waters;
cursed is their portion in the land;
he turns not toward the way of the vineyards.

The womb forgets him; the worm feeds sweetly on him;
he is no longer remembered,
and iniquity is broken like a tree.

Raised up for a little, then they are not;
they are laid low, gathered in like everyone else;
like the tops of the ears of grain they are lopped.

See, these are the outskirts of his ways;
and how faint a whisper of a word we hear of him!
But the thunder of his mighty deeds—who can understand?”

He breaks open a shaft away from where men sojourn;{b}
forgotten by the foot,
they hang far from men; they swing to and fro.

Through want and hard famine they are gaunt;
they gnaw the dry ground,
the gloom{b} of waste and desolation.

On my right hand the rabble{g} rises up;
they thrust away my feet
and cast up against me their ways of destruction.

They break up my path;
they promote my calamity;
no one helps them.{h}

Terrors are rolled upon me;
my nobility is chased off as by the wind,
and my welfare is gone by like a cloud.

if my steps have strayed from the road,
and my heart has followed after my eyes,
or any stain has stuck to my palms,

let thorns come up instead of wheat,
and stinking weeds{i} instead of barley.”
The words of Job are ended.
